 "report": "Particle A42-spiral-e000-d055: cascade (k=7)\n\nWhat it is: a particle of mass 2.3 and radius 1.64 with 7 ports on its rim. Each port is a stick-slip contact with a dead zone (mean 0.35) and a 4-dim charge; 10 of its port pairs attract each other strongly (so copies can bind port to port). Inside, 14 couplings (50% inhibitory) pass a push from port to port; it settles in 3 sweeps with slip rate 0.96 and keeps its state (memory).\nHow it assembles (32 copies, 2 worlds): 1.28 bonds per particle, largest molecule 6.0 particles, 7.5 left free.\n\nIts matter vs an ESN of the same size (readout only, both with tuned input scale); controls: no bonds, random bonds:\n  memcap   0.098 vs ESN 0.999\u00b10.000  [below]  no bonds 0.089  random bonds 0.097\n  xor      0.574 vs ESN 0.514\u00b10.003  [beat]  no bonds 0.627  random bonds 0.534\n  narma10  0.284 vs ESN 0.844\u00b10.001  [below]  no bonds 0.272  random bonds 0.303\n  latch    0.863 vs ESN 0.678\u00b10.003  [beat]  no bonds 0.864  random bonds 0.928\n  mnist14  0.520 vs ESN 0.830\u00b10.007  [below]  no bonds 0.489  random bonds 0.576\n\nGood at: xor, latch.\nIts assembly does not matter: the particles do as well unbonded, so the useful part is the single particle's own stick-slip memory.\nUse (a guess): combining recent events nonlinearly (temporal feature detection); holding a value until it is overwritten, with nothing trained but the readout: a physical memory element for a recurrent or reservoir system."
